==================
   Introduction
==================

                --- What is StickMan? ---

         StickMan is a BBS Door. Unlike most doors, StickMan is an arcade-
        style game. In StickMan, players walk, jump, climb & fall in an
        effort to avoid dangerous monsters and grab bonus points, health
        power-ups etc. StickMan comes with several levels built in.

         Setup is simple, and StickMan is compatable with any BBS that uses a
        fossil driver, and can generate a Door.Sys, DorInfo.Def, or Chain.Txt.
        Parameters can also be passed on the command line.

         High scores bulletins are generated in ANSI color and plain text.

         The Registered version of StickMan loads external levels, and
        comes with level & monster editors, so you can create your own
        custom levels and monsters. Registered Sysops also receive several
        extra levels.


                --- Liability ---

         This program is provided "as is" and is without warranty of any kind.
        No liability shall be assumed by Total System Support for direct or
        consequential damage caused by your use or misuse of this software.
                              

                --- Copyright ---

         StickMan, and all contents thereof, including but not limited to
        screens, monsters, menus, levels, documentation and software, are
        copyright (c) 1995 by Total System Support. Unauthorized duplication,
        distribution or use is illegal and will result in criminal prosecution
        and/or civil litagation.


                --- Shareware ---

         Shareware offers a try before you buy approach to software marketing.
        The user receives a "Demo" or shareware version of the software, which
        they are free to try out for a limited time period. Often the share-
        ware version is missing some features, or is limited in some other way.

         If the user decides to purchase the software, they "Register" it.
        That is, they pay the author, and they receive the "registered" version
        of the software.


                --- Distribution ---

         You may distribute the shareware version of StickMan, provided that
        this archive is not modified in any way, and that you do not charge
        money for the software. Disk vendors may charge a nominal ($3 or less)
        fee for the disk the software is distributed on. BBS Sysops may provide
        the shareware version of StickMan for download.

         You may NOT distribute the registered version of StickMan under ANY
        circumstances.


                --- Registration ---

         After the one month trial period, if you wish to continue using
        StickMan, you must register. Registration costs very little : $15.

         Registered Sysops will receive the following:
                - the latest registered version of StickMan
                - the ability to load external levels
                - the external level editor
                - the monster editor
                - priority tech support

         Shareware StickMan will stop functioning after your one month trial
        period expires.

         For details on how to register, see "Order.Frm".


                --- Quick Start ---

         To try playing StickMan right away, use local mode via command line
        parameters. The simplest way is to type "STKMN_SW /PORT:0". This
        will let you play using the name "Annon. User". For name parameters
        see the "Using Command Line Parameters" section below.


                --- What's New? ---

         StickMan ver 1.11 added "Terminal Type" to options screen. Users
        should select "Type 2" if their keystrokes are delayed durring game-
        play. This causes a periodic delay in data sent to the terminal, which
        some terminals require before they will send the users keystrokes.


         Added features in StickMan ver 1.1 include:
                User can see time remaining in door.
                Help screen accessable durring game play.
                Changed colors in high scores.




==================
   Installation
==================

                --- System Requirements ---

            - FOSSIL driver
            - 275k Available Base Memory
            - 300k Disk Space (approx.)
            - Dos 5.0 or higher ( previous versions not tested )
            - BBS Software capable of running a door, passing data via:
                                  "Door.Sys" style dropfile
                               or "DoorInfo.Def" style dropfile
                               or "Chain.Txt" style dropfile
                               or command line parameters


                --- Files ---

         This archive should contian the following files:

               sm1.ans   Intro Screen
               sm2.ans   Main Menu Screen
               sm3.ans   Options Screen
               sm4.ans   Choose Level Screen
               sm5.ans   Top Ten Screen
               sm6.ans   High Scores Screen
               sm7.ans   User Scores Screen
               sm8.ans   Instruction Menu Screen
               sm9.ans   Instruction Screen
              sm10.ans   Instruction Screen
              sm11.ans   Instruction Screen
              sm12.ans   Instruction Screen
              sm13.ans   Instruction Screen
              sm14.ans   Instruction Screen
              sm15.ans   Instruction Screen
              sm16.ans   Instruction Screen
              sm17.ans   Instruction Screen
              sm18.ans   Header for Scores Bulletin
              sm18.txt   Header for Scores Bulletin
             order.frm   Order Form
          stickman.doc   This Documentation
          stickman.lvs   Levels File
          stkmn_sw.exe   The Program
           file_id.diz   Description File


         StickMan will also create the following files when used:

          stickman.log   Record of user log-in/out, errors, etc.
          stickman.usr   User Data (scores, options, etc.)
            topten.scr   Top Ten Scores Data


                --- Setup ---

         Setup for StickMan consists of two parts, installing files and
        setting up your BBS to recognize StickMan.

         The first part is very easy. Just create a new directory, and copy
        all the files from this archive into it. That's all!

         The second part is going to depend on which BBS software you are
        using. Consult the documentation for your BBS software on how to
        set up a door. See also "Using Door.Sys", "Using DorInfo.Def",
        "Using Chain.Txt", and/or "Using Command Line Parameters" below.


                --- Bulletins ---

         StickMan generates two bulletins every time a user exits the door.
                        - SM_Scores.ANS  - color version top ten scores
                        - SM_Scores.TXT  - mono version top ten scores

         You may freely delete, move or copy these files. StickMan creates
        these files but does not read, or rely on them.


                --- Using Door.Sys ---

         If your BBS software uses a "Door.Sys" dropfile to configure doors,
        you can use the "/Drop:" parameter to tell StickMan where to find
        this file.

        Example: If the Door.Sys file will be located in "C:\BBS\NODE1", use

           "STKMN_SW /DROP:C:\BBS\NODE1"
                        or
           "STKMN_SW /DROP:C:\BBS\NODE1\"
                        or
           "STKMN_SW /DROP:C:\BBS\NODE1\DOOR.SYS"

         StickMan will get the fossil port, baudrate, user name and node number
        from the Door.Sys file.


                --- Using DorInfo.Def ---

         If your BBS software uses a "DorInfo.Def" dropfile to configure doors,
        you can use the "/DOR:" parameter to tell StickMan where to find this
        file. Since DorInfo style dropfiles include the node number in the
        filename, you must include the full filename.

        Example: If the DoorInfo file will be named "DOORINFO1.DEF", and will
        be located in "C:\BBS\DOORS" use:

                "STKMN_SW /DOR:C:\BBS\DOORS\DORINFO1.DEF"

         StickMan will get the fossil port, baudrate, and user name from the
        DorInfo file.


                --- Using Chain.Txt ---

         If your BBS software uses a "Chain.Txt" dropfile to configure doors,
        you can use the "/Chain:" parameter to tell StickMan where to find
        this file.

        Example: If the Chain.Txt file will be located in "C:\BBS\NODE1", use

           "STKMN_SW /CHAIN:C:\BBS\NODE1"
                        or
           "STKMN_SW /CHAIN:C:\BBS\NODE1\"
                        or
           "STKMN_SW /CHAIN:C:\BBS\NODE1\CHAIN.TXT"

         StickMan will get the fossil port, baudrate, and user name from the
        Chain.Txt file.


                --- Using Command Line Parameters ---

         If your BBS software uses none of the above dropfile formats, or
        you need to override settings in the drop file, command line parameters
        can be used. StickMan supports the following parameters:

                Param   Example         Meaning
                -------------------------------------------------------------
                /PORT:  /PORT:1         fossil port to use (1 based) 0 = local
                /BAUD:  /BAUD:38400     baud rate to use (optional)
                /MIN:   /MIN:30         time user has remaining (default = 15)
                /F:     /F:Joe          user's first name
                /L:     /L:Smith        user's last name

         Parameters can come in any order. These parameters can be used along
        with the dropfile parameters mentioned in previous paragraphs. If the
        "/Port:" parameter is used, the dropfile will be ignored. If a dropfile
        is used along with "/BAUD:" and/or "/MIN:" the command line will
        override the dropfile. The "/F:" and "/L:" parameters only apply if
        no dropfile is used.
        

                --- Locked BaudRate ---

         If you are using a locked baudrate (above 38400), any baudrate passed
        to StickMan will be ignored by the fossil driver. StickMan does not
        touch the com port directly in any way.



===============
   Game Play
===============

                --- StartUp ---

         When the user enters the door, a title screen (sm1.ans) is displayed.
        This screen gives the user something to look at while StickMan gets
        a few things set up. The setup delay is very brief (2-3 sec.), and the
        user is then prompted to "press any key".


                --- Menus ---

         StickMan features several menus, but they all basicly work the same
        way. The up & down arrows, or "8" for up, and "2" for down, are used
        to move the twirling line. Once you have moved to the item you want,
        press Enter to select it. Select "Instructions" from the main menu for
        more info about specific menus.


                --- Levels ---

         The shareware version of StickMan comes with 6 levels. Some have only
        one screen, some have 4-5 screens. Each level must be completed in
        sequence. Once a user has completed several levels, they may use the
        "Choose Level" item from the main menu to select a previous level.


                --- Movement ---

         Durring gameplay, the user may walk, climb, jump and shoot using the
        following keys:

                        4 or Left arrow  = Walk Left
                        6 or Right arrow = Walk Right

                        8 or Up arrow    = Climb Up
                        2 or Down arrow  = Climb Down

                        7 or Home key    = Jump Left
                        9 or PgUp key    = Jump Right

                        , or <           = Shoot Left
                        . or >           = Shoot Right

         For more detailed information, see "Moving Around"
        in the "Instructions" menu.


                --- Objects ---

         You will find these objects in various StickMan Levels:

                        › ...... Bonus (worth one point)

                        ï ...... Ammo (three shots worth)

                        + ...... First Aid Kit

                        ^ ...... Spike (don't step on any of these!)

                        ê ...... Exit (white omega)

                        ê ...... Teleport (other color omegas)

         For information on structures, monsters, etc. see the "Instructions"
        menu.


                --- High Scores ---

         StickMan keeps track of each players total score, as well as how
        many of each type of monster a player has killed. The Top Ten
        bulletins are based entirely on the player's total score.


================
   Tech Notes
================

                --- Hanging Up ---

         Pressing "Alt-H" localy will cause StickMan to hang up on the user.


                --- StickMan.Log ---

         StickMan.Log is a plain text file that is created or appended to.
        User Logons, Logoffs and program crashes are noted in the log, along
        with a date & time. StickMan does not read this file, so you can freely
        delete, edit, or truncate this file.


                --- Changing the ANSI Files ---

         Please do not distribute modified versions of StickMan. But, if
        you would like to customize your own installation, you may modify
        the SM*.ans files. The "Files" section above shows which Ansi is which.

        A few do's and don'ts:
         do: Make sure each new file starts with an ansi clear screen (Esc[2J)
         do: Include a "changed" notice, for example: "New Screen by Joe Sysop"
         do: Make sure the new file makes sense.
      don't: Use more than 23 lines.
      don't: Plagarise (copy other people's work)
      don't: Break any laws. (copyright, libel, obsecnity, etc.)
      don't: Make false claims regarding copyright or origin of StickMan.
      don't: Change or omit the name "StickMan", or "T.S.S." etc.
      don't: Attempt to obscure or hide the copyright notice (see below).
      don't: Do anything your mother wouldn't approve of.

         The Ansi Screens will be overwritten with the menu twirler and the
        copyright notice. The twirler is dark gray on light gray background.
        The copyright notice is black on a light gray background. Anything
        you put in a replacement ansi wil be overwritten by these items.


                --- Changing the StickMan.Lvs File ---

         The Levels file is a plain text file that defines what levels are
        used, and in which order. Since the shareware version does not use
        external map files, you should NOT change this file in any way.


                --- Door.Sys Format ---

         StickMan expects the Door.Sys file to be a
        plain text file with the following information:

                Line 1  : "COMn" n = fossil port (1 based), this number must
                                 be the fourth character on this line, all
                                 other characters on this line are ignored.

                Line 2  : BaudRate, just the number, nothing else.

                Line 4  : Node Number, "0" = local. This number is used in
                          the log file, and on the status bar.

                Line 10 : User's Name.

                Line 19 : Time the user has left, in minuets.

         The node number is not crucial, but all other items are.


                --- DorInfo.Def Format ---

         StickMan expects the DorInfo.Def file to be a
        plain text file with the following information:

                Line 4  : "COMn" n = fossil port (1 based), this number must
                                 be the fourth character on this line, all
                                 other characters on this line are ignored.

                Line 5  : BaudRate, followed by a blank space, and anything
                          else on this line is ignored.

                Line 7  : User's First Name
                Line 8  : User's Last Name

                Line 12 : Time the user has left, in minuets.

                Line 13 : Node Number, "0" = local. This number is used in
                          the log file, and on the status bar.

         The node number is not crucial, but all other items are.


                --- Chain.Txt Format ---

         StickMan expects the Chain.Txt file to be a
        plain text file with the following information:

                Line 3  : User's Name

                Line 16 : Time the user has left, in seconds.

                Line 20 : BaudRate, just the number, nothing else.

                Line 21 : Fossil port (1 based), just the number.

                Line 13 : Node Number, "0" = local. This number is used in
                          the log file, and on the status bar.

         The node number is not crucial, but all other items are.


                --- Future Features ---

         If StickMan is well received, future versions may be released.
        Some features we may be adding include:
                        - On-Line Level & Monster Editors
                        - More types of Scores
                        - Flying Monsters
                        - Monsters that Shoot
                        - Multi-Player Support ?
                        - User-Base Editor
                        - Scrolling Levels
                        - Elevators
                        - Not Require Fossil

         If you have more features to suggest, bug reports, comments, etc.,
        send e-mail to bill.reamy@tssol.com


